course: adult-life
lesson: 19
title: A new baby in the house
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A newborn baby changes a whole house. It has a tiny stomach, so it needs feeding every two or three hours, day and night, and crying is the only way it can say what it needs.
explain: Parents get very little sleep, so they can be tired, grumpy and forgetful for a few months, and visitors seem to talk only about the baby.
explain: In Vancouver, Dad yawns over his cereal, the changing bag by the door is stuffed with nappies, and Mum calls from the sofa, "She's feeding, can you give us a minute?" Nine-year-old Josh feels a bit left out, and that is normal.
explain: Babies join families in other ways too: some children are adopted for good, and some foster children come to stay for a while. Parents get time off work, called maternity and paternity leave, to look after a new baby.
fact: A newborn baby needs feeding every two or three hours, day and night, because its stomach is tiny.
fact: Crying is a baby's only way to say it is hungry, wet, cold or tired, and the night waking slowly gets better over a few months.
fact: Parents of a new baby are very tired, and tired people can snap or forget things; it is not a change in how they feel about you.
fact: Feeling jealous of a new baby is very common, and parents still love the older child just as much.
fact: A christening is a church ceremony where a baby is welcomed into the Christian church and given its names, with water on its head.
fact: Godparents are chosen at a christening to take a special interest in the child as it grows.
fact: Adoption makes a child, by law, a permanent member of a new family, and the adoptive parents are the child's real parents.
fact: Fostering is looking after a child from another family for a while, when their own parents cannot cope for now.
fact: Paternity leave is time off work for a new father; in Britain it is usually two weeks.
fact: Maternity leave is time off work for a new mother; in Britain it can last up to a year, often on lower pay.
fact: Babies wear nappies, which are changed six to ten times a day, and nappy cream stops sore skin.
fact: Babies feed from the breast or a bottle, often for twenty minutes or more, and they need calm to settle.
exercise: Why does a new baby wake and cry every two or three hours at night? | Its tiny stomach needs feeding very often | It is having bad dreams about monsters every night | It does not like its new family | It wants the lights switched on
exercise: What can a baby's crying mean? | It is hungry, wet, cold or tired | It is always cross with its parents | It has learnt a bad habit on purpose to annoy everyone | It is trying to say a first word
exercise: Dad yawns, "We haven't had a full night's sleep in weeks." Why? | The baby wakes them many times a night | Dad has been working night shifts at the hospital | The neighbours have been very noisy | Dad has been watching TV all night
exercise: Mum snaps at you over nothing since the baby came. What is the most likely reason? | She is very tired, not loving you less | She now loves the new baby much more than you | You have done something terribly wrong | She has decided to be strict from now on
exercise: Every visitor only asks about the baby and you feel cross. What is this feeling? | Jealousy, which most big brothers and sisters feel | Hunger, because dinner is late again | Illness, so you should stay in bed | Boredom, because babies are dull
exercise: You feel jealous of the new baby. What is a good thing to do? | Tell a parent how you feel, in words | Hide the baby's dummy so it cries | Pretend you are a baby too | Keep it in and never say anything
exercise: Gran says, "The christening is on Sunday." What is a christening? | A church ceremony welcoming a baby and giving its names | A party for the baby's first birthday with a big cake and candles | A visit to the doctor for jabs | The day the baby comes home from hospital
exercise: What are godparents? | Grown-ups chosen to take a special interest in the child | The baby's grandparents on Mum's side of the family, who live nearby | Priests who look after the church | The parents of the baby's best friend
exercise: Your cousin says, "I was adopted. Mum and Dad chose me." Which is true? | His mum and dad are his real parents in every way | He is only staying with them for a while until he grows up | His real family is somewhere else | He is not really part of the family
exercise: Mum says, "A foster child is coming to live with us for a while." What does it mean? | A child whose parents cannot cope will stay for a time | A new baby has been born to Mum | A cousin is coming for the whole summer holiday to stay | A child is coming to be adopted at once and stay forever
exercise: The foster child is quiet and grumpy at first. Why might that be? | Being in a new home is scary for them | They do not like your family at all | They have been very naughty at home | They want to go on holiday with you
exercise: "Dad's on paternity leave for two weeks." What is that? | Time off work for a new dad | A holiday Dad has won abroad | A course Dad is doing at work | Time off because Dad is ill
exercise: "Mum's on maternity leave." What is that? | Time off work for a new mum to care for the baby | Mum has lost her job for good and won't go back | Mum is working from home every day | Mum is on holiday without the family
exercise: Why might there be fewer treats while Mum is on maternity leave? | Her pay is often lower during the leave | Treats are bad for new babies, even if they don't eat them | Babies eat all the family's sweets | Shops stop selling treats to families
exercise: What is in a changing bag? | Nappies, wipes and nappy cream | Toys, games and colouring books | Clean clothes for Mum and Dad | Snacks and drinks for the older kids
exercise: Mum is on the sofa: "She's feeding, can you give us a minute?" What should you do? | Leave them in peace or sit quietly nearby | Turn the TV up loud so the baby can listen | Ask Mum to play a game right away | Pick the baby up to help it eat
test: Overheard: "He was up at one, three, half four and six. I'm a zombie." Who is "he" most likely? | A newborn baby waking to feed | A burglar trying to get in through the back door | A dog barking at the postman | A teenager playing video games
test: Your little sister says, "The baby cries 'cos it hates us." Put her right. | Crying is how babies say what they need | She's right, the baby is cross with all of us | She's right, babies hate older children | It cries because it wants to go back to hospital
test: Dad, bleary: "Sorry mate, I forgot your football, I'm knackered." What is going on? | Dad is worn out from the baby's nights | Dad does not care about football any more | Dad has been out at parties every night | Dad has stopped loving his older children
test: Gossip at the gate: "Poor Josh, everyone just coos over the baby, he's got his nose right out of joint." What does it mean? | Josh feels left out and jealous | Josh has broken his nose playing football in the yard | Josh has a cold and a blocked nose | Josh is proud of being a big brother
test: A boy in Dublin says, "I'm the baby's godfather, so I'm her dad now." Put him right. | A godparent takes an interest; he is not her dad | He is right, godparents become the baby's parents | He is right, but only on Sundays | A godfather is the baby's grandad
test: Your mate says, "Liam's adopted, so his mum's not his proper mum." Put him right. | His adoptive mum is his real mum in every way | He's right, only birth mums count as real mums | He's right, until Liam turns eighteen | Liam has to go back home one day
test: Mum on the phone: "She might be with us a week or a few years, depends how things go at home." What is she talking about? | A foster child staying with them | A new baby born in the family | An adopted child who stays forever | A cousin on a summer visit
test: Mum, checking the bank app: "No takeaways for a bit, love, I'm on maternity pay now." Why? | Pay is often lower during maternity leave | Takeaways are bad for the new baby | Mum has been sacked from her job | The baby has spent all the money
test: Dad, holding his nose: "Nappy number nine today. Pass the wipes and the cream, quick." What is normal here? | A baby needs many nappy changes a day | The baby is ill and needs a doctor straight away | Dad is using far too many nappies | The baby should be using the toilet
test: Your sister insists, "Paternity leave means Dad's been fired." Put her right. | It is time off work to be with the new baby | She is right, Dad has lost his job | She is right, but it is only for a month | It is a holiday Dad has to pay for
test: Gran: "Sunday, church at eleven, he's having water poured on his head, so behave." What is happening? | The baby's christening | The baby's first swimming lesson | A wedding in the family | The baby's first birthday party
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the frequent nocturnal waking of a newborn infant? | It arises from the infant's need for frequent feeding, its stomach being small | It arises from the infant's dislike of its family | It arises from a deliberately acquired bad habit | It is invariably a sign of serious illness requiring immediate admission to hospital
exam: Identify the principal distinction between adoption and fostering. | Adoption is permanent by law, whereas fostering is ordinarily temporary | Fostering is permanent by law, whereas adoption is temporary | Adoption applies only to newborn infants | Fostering confers the status of birth parent
exam: Pursuant to the provisions customary in Britain, paternity leave is best characterised as which of the following? | A period of leave from employment, usually two weeks, granted to a new father | A period of unpaid holiday taken abroad | A period of leave granted only to mothers | A permanent cessation of employment
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning jealousy felt by an older sibling towards a newborn. | It is very common and does not signify that the older child is loved less | It is rare and signifies a serious problem | It signifies that the parents prefer the newborn to the older child in every respect | It ought to be concealed from the parents
